---
import { getCollection, getEntry, render } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { parts, chapterNumber, appendix, frontMatter, paper } from '../../data/book-structure';
import { printEditionPublic, printEditionPath } from '../../data/print-edition';
import { priceIncVat, kr } from '../../data/printed-copy';

const preface = await getEntry('chapters', 'preface');
if (!preface) throw new Error('Preface entry missing');
const { Content } = await render(preface);

// Subject nouns come from the chapter files rather than from book-structure, so
// the topic shown here and the one on the chapter page cannot drift apart.
const topics = new Map(
  (await getCollection('chapters')).map((c) => [c.id, c.data.topic]).filter(([, t]) => t)
);

---

<BaseLayout title={`${paper.title} — ${paper.subtitle}`} description={paper.tagline}>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="kicker">{paper.eyebrow}</p>
    <h1>{paper.title}<span class="hero-sub">{paper.subtitle}</span></h1>
    <p class="hero-tagline">{paper.tagline}</p>
    <p class="hero-thesis">
      Organizations don't become slow because people work slowly.
      They become slow because friction accumulates faster than value is created.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      Organizational Flow is an organization's ability to continuously create value. Every organization has
      Organizational Flow — it is a property you hold a position on, not a state you arrive at. It is not a
      framework or a methodology. It is a way of seeing.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      Structural friction determines how much of an organization's potential is actually realized.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      We learned that the systems we build are never truly finished. They evolve continuously and must be
      cultivated over time. Organizations are no different. If one is designed for continuous evolution while
      the other is designed for completion, structural friction is inevitable. The systems we build and the
      organizations that build them must therefore be cultivated together.
    </p>
    <p class="hero-axiom">
      Organizational Flow rises as information, decisions, execution and learning move through enduring
      capabilities with clear ownership — and falls as structural friction accumulates faster than value
      is created.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-friction">
      <p>
        A paper this long is itself friction — a wall of material standing between you and the two or three
        parts that would actually help you on Monday. This one argues that friction should be named rather
        than tolerated, so: five things exist to take some of it off you.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/organizational-flow/the-ten-minute-version/">The Ten-Minute Version</a> — the whole
          argument, standing on its own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/organizational-flow/self-assessment/">A Closer Look</a> — eighteen questions that point
          you at the chapters matching whatever is currently creaking, so you needn't guess from a contents
          page.
        </li>
        <li>
          {/* The player lives on the Print Edition route. If that page is ever
              hidden, the audio needs a home of its own first. */}
          <a href={printEditionPath}>Audio</a> — listen while you run, drive or do the washing up.
          Costs you no evening.
        </li>
        <li>
          <button class="friction-buy" type="button" data-open-order>A printed copy</button> — if
          you read better on paper, or you want this sitting on somebody's table where it has a
          fighting chance of being read instead of spending eternity in an open browser tab.
          {kr(priceIncVat)} plus postage, printed on demand.
        </li>
        <li>
          Chapters that more or less stand alone — take a little of Part I with you, since that is where
          the words get settled, and after that jump wherever you like. Part IV is
          the <em>how do I actually do this</em> end of it. Nobody needs to read the whole thing in order, and
          I would rather you didn't try it in one sitting.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="hero-friction-close">
        I try to live as I learn. Working out which chapters matter to you shouldn't be your job.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* printEditionPublic hides the online edition. The PDF is no longer
        offered here at all — it lives behind the licence line in the footer,
        for anyone who goes looking. */}
    <p class="hero-action">
      {printEditionPublic && (
        <a class="download-paper" href={printEditionPath}>Read it all</a>
      )}
      <button class="download-paper" type="button" data-open-order>Get your copy</button>
      <span class="download-note">
        Or just carry on down the page and read a part at a time.
      </span>
    </p>
    <p class="hero-aside">
      Or start with the question the paper is really asking:
      <a href="/organizational-flow/self-assessment/">where is your organization losing movement?</a>
    </p>
    <div class="horizon"></div>
  </section>

  <main class="reading" id="main">
    <section class="block preface" id="preface">
      <p class="kicker">Preface</p>
      <Content />
    </section>

    <hr class="chapter-divider" />

    <div class="toc-thesis">
      <p>Organizations become slow when friction accumulates faster than value is created.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="toc-part">
      <p class="kicker">Preface</p>
      {preface.data.dek && <p class="toc-question">{preface.data.dek}</p>}
      <ol class="toc-cols">
        {/* The Preface has no page of its own — it is the body of this one —
            so it links back up the page rather than out to a route. */}
        <li>
          <a class="toc-chapter" href="#preface">
            <span>Preface</span>
          </a>
        </li>
        {frontMatter.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a class="toc-chapter" href={`/organizational-flow/${item.slug}/`}>
              <span>{item.title}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>

    {parts.map((part) => (
      <div class="toc-part">
        <p class="kicker">Part {part.id} — {part.title}</p>
        <p class="toc-question">{part.question}</p>
        <ol class="toc-cols">
          {part.chapters.map((chapter) => (
            <li>
              {chapter.status === 'published' ? (
                <a class="toc-chapter" href={`/organizational-flow/${chapter.slug}/`}>
                  <span>
                    <span class="toc-num">{chapterNumber(chapter.slug)}</span>{chapter.title}
                    {topics.get(chapter.slug) && (
                      <span class="toc-topic">{topics.get(chapter.slug)}</span>
                    )}
                  </span>
                </a>
              ) : (
                <span class="toc-chapter is-upcoming">
                  <span>{chapter.title}</span>
                  <span class="toc-status">Coming</span>
                </span>
              )}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    ))}

    <div class="toc-part">
      <p class="kicker">Appendix</p>
      <ol class="toc-cols">
        {appendix.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a class="toc-chapter" href={`/organizational-flow/${item.slug}/`}>
              <span>
                {item.title}
                {item.topic && <span class="toc-topic">{item.topic}</span>}
              </span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer label="Organizational Flow — an ongoing work" />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // Report downloads into the same Cronitor property as pageviews.
  function track(event: string) {
    const cronitor = (window as unknown as { cronitor?: (...args: unknown[]) => void }).cronitor;
    if (typeof cronitor === 'function') cronitor('track', event);
  }
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-download-paper]').forEach((link) => {
    link.addEventListener('click', () => track('Download PDF'));
  });
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-printed-copy]').forEach((link) => {
    link.addEventListener('click', () => track('Ask About Printed Copy'));
  });
</script>

<style>
  /* Its own quiet card, not a marginalia aside — a direct address deserves
     the full measure, not a narrow column beside the Preface. */
  .reader-note {
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin: 2.4rem auto 0;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.6rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--mist);
    border-radius: 0.6rem;
    background: var(--card);
  }
  .reader-note-label {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gold);
  }
  .reader-note p:not(.reader-note-label) {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--bark);
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }
  .reader-note p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* The paper naming its own friction. Sits between the argument and the
     download, because a first-time reader should meet the ways out before
     they meet the size of the thing. */
  .hero-friction {
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin: 2.6rem auto 0;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.7rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--mist);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: var(--card);
    text-align: left;
  }
  .hero-friction p { margin: 0 0 1rem; color: var(--bark); font-size: 0.96rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .hero-friction ul { margin: 0; padding: 0 0 0 1.1rem; }
  .hero-friction li {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    color: var(--bark);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .hero-friction a { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--moss); }
  .hero-friction a:hover { color: var(--moss); }
  .hero-friction-close {
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0 !important;
    padding-top: 0.9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--mist);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .hero-action {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
  }
  .hero-action .download-note {
    flex: 1 0 100%;
    text-align: center;
  }
  /* A button keeps the platform's own fill and text colour unless both are
     cleared — which is what made it unreadable against the dark palette.
     Deliberately no `font` shorthand here: this selector outranks
     .download-paper below, so `font: inherit` silently overrode the family
     and size and printed this button half again as large as the link beside
     it, in the body serif. The class sets the type for both. */
  button.download-paper {
    color: var(--ink);
    background: none;
    -webkit-appearance: none;
    appearance: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .download-paper {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    /* Stated rather than inherited: a link picks up the body's line-height
       and a button keeps the platform's `normal`, which left the two pills
       three pixels apart in height. */
    line-height: 1.4;
    /* Both pills sit at one width so neither reads as the louder offer —
       the paper is given away, and the order button should not out-shout
       the invitation to read it. Sized to the longer of the two labels. */
    min-width: 10.5rem;
    text-align: center;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--bark);
    border-radius: 20px;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.4rem;
    transition: color .2s ease, border-color .2s ease;
  }
  .download-paper:hover {
    color: var(--moss);
    border-color: var(--moss);
  }
  .download-note {
    font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--bark);
  }
  .download-note a {
    color: var(--bark);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--mist);
  }
  .download-note a:hover { color: var(--moss); border-color: var(--moss); }
  .toc-num {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 1.9rem;
    font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--bark);
  }
  /* Part II titles are written to raise curiosity, so the subject noun rides
     alongside them here — otherwise the contents page stops being scannable. */
  .toc-topic {
    margin-left: 0.5rem;
    font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bark);
    opacity: 0.75;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* The Editor's Note is far longer than a chapter marginalia; pinning it
     would leave it taller than the viewport on most screens. */
  .preface :global(.marginalia-col) {
    position: static !important;
  }
  .preface :global(.marginalia-text + .marginalia-text) {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
  }
  .hero-thesis {
    max-width: 36rem;
    margin: 0 auto 1.6rem;
    font-family: 'Fraunces', serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 2.2vw, 1.3rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .toc-thesis {
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin: 0 auto 3rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .toc-thesis p {
    font-family: 'Fraunces', serif;
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 auto;
    max-width: 34rem;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .toc-question {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin: -0.4rem 0 1rem;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bark);
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .hero-aside {
    margin: 1.1rem auto 0;
    max-width: 32rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--bark);
  }
  .hero-aside a {
    color: var(--bark);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--mist);
    transition: color .2s ease, border-color .2s ease;
  }
  .hero-aside a:hover {
    color: var(--moss);
    border-color: var(--moss);
  }
</style>
